{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prop-substrate-radial-coordinate","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Proposition","doco:Section"],"label":"prop:substrate-radial-coordinate","kind":"proposition","title":"Substrate parameter as the radial coordinate","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-contact-affine-chart","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-fermion-boson-decomposition","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-quantum-planck-constant","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/ex-q-reeb-flow"],"proofs":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prf-substrate-radial-coordinate"],"tags":["substrate","projective","boson","fermion"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.substrate_radial_coordinate","text":"substrate-radial-coordinate.md","leanSource":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/substrate-radial-coordinate.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prop:substrate-radial-coordinate\"></a><p>Within the contact affine chart <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">U_\\xi</code>, the substrate parameter <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code> governs the radial coordinate. As <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\to \\infty</code> in the deep-quantum limit, the affine coordinate <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Z \\to 0</code>. Conversely, as <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q \\to 1</code> at the classical boundary, <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Z \\to \\infty</code>. The <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">q</code>-deformed Reeb flow rotates the phase of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">Z</code> according to:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  \\mathcal{L}_\\xi Z =  J\\hbar_q Z,</code></pre>\n<p>where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\hbar_q = 1 - q^{-1}</code> is the quantum Planck constant and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">J</code> is the complex structure on the horizontal distribution <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H</code>.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/substrate-radial-coordinate.md","heading":"Proposition","number":"3.6.3"}
